            Debug.Assert(Old_index <= New_arrListLastIndexOfX, "The index is greater than the last index of the element in the array.");
        }
    }
}

As you can see the PUT_SetContract method only changes the value of the array at the given index. In addition, it checks some properties of the array. The properties to check are the number of elements in the array (Count), the index where the element (x) is located in the array using IndexOf, and whether the array contains the element (x). If the properties of the array are valid before and after executing the operation, the test can conclude that the operation was correct. Otherwise, there may be an issue in the ArrayList class that needs to be investigated further.

The method makes use of PexAssumeUnderTest on an ArrayList instance, which implies to the analyzer that the instance (arrList) should be taken as the target under test. Thus, the analyzer understands that you are not manipulating non-public members or fields in the ArrayList class and focuses on how the public members and properties react to changes in a test case. This allows the analyzer to provide meaningful information by reducing the surface of the potential test cases to test.

This method also uses the framework's observed values in PexObserve to show the initial and resulting state of the array, and the initial and resulting state of the element and the array properties, which can provide insights from the perspective of other developers.

Also note that, this is just sample code, and your code may have more sophisticated assertions or preconditions. 

However, your original intention was unit testing, which is more aligned with unit testing frameworks (e.g., NUnit, XUnit). The Pex testing features offered in contracts are more for generating test inputs for other methods (e.g., methods like Insert, Remove, Clear) within similar data structures so that the edge cases can be covered during tests for contracts. Pex can generate values that you can use for these tests, making your tests more useful (e.g., it covers edge cases).

So, in summary, PUT_SetContract is a generic unit testing method that uses Pex features in contracts to test the behavior of method(s) (which is not in current context). Pex's features can be used effectively while unit testing. But, in the context of current problem, Pex's feature is more applicable for checking the behavior of Insert, Remove, Clear methods on ArrayList, or methods modifying Array's properties.

Hope this helps in more understanding about this topic.

A side note: Pex is based on path-coverage which does not guarantee 100% coverage of your code. It's a form of random mutation testing and works best with methods with a non-deterministic execution like methods that perform IO operations or methods that use random or uncertain parameters. For methods that are predictable, consider using property-based testing (e.g., NUnit's [Property] attribute) which provides the confidence in a sound coverage guarantee.
In some cases, you might even use a combination of Pex and other techniques. For example, writing test methods that use Pex to generate test data and verify the results, as well as using NUnit's attributes - like [Property] and [Range], for predictable paths.
As the Pex docs suggest, the key to efficient, effective, and meaningful tests using Pex is to understand the difference between the test cases you want to generate and the test cases that you want to run.

A caveat of Pex is that it is harder to understand the behavior of complex code. The best way to use Pex for complex code is to create a simple test target class that you can use to test the more complex code using Pex. By isolating the complex part you can better understand it and create meaningful test cases.
Further reading:

"Path testing with Static Analysis" paper (http://research.microsoft.com/pubs/65444/msr-tr-2007-65.pdf) offers a practical look on the challenges with automatic testing tools and why careful design is beneficial to generate effective test inputs.

Pex's "Path Testing with Static Analysis" talk (https://channel9.msdn.com/Shows/Code-Mesh-Open/Path-Testing-with-Static-Analysis) offers a very practical look into various scenarios of applying unit tests and Pex. It covers Pex-aware workflows, how to use Pex with other testing frameworks, and even a sample implementation of a custom method call (or Pex step) provider by using reflection. 

