```csharp
[TestMethod]
[TestCategory("PUT")]
[PexMethod(ActionVerb.Set, "ArrayList", "this[int]", new string[] { "x" }, new string[] { "index" })]
public void PUT_Set_ArrayList_this_int_int(
    [PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList,
    int x,
    int index)
{
    AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index < arrList.Count);

    int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int Old_x = x;
    int Old_index = index;
    int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    arrList[index] = x;

    int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
    int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
    int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
    bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListCount, New_arrListCount);
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_x, New_arrListIndexOfX);
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_index, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListIndexOfX, New_arrListIndexOfX);
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListLastIndexOfX, New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
    Assert.AreEqual(Old_arrListContainsX, New_arrListContainsX);
}
```   

This is an interesting example of how Pex can be used to thoroughly test a list's behavior after a modification using Set operation. The test case leverages a property in the test framework's [AssumePrecondition](/visualstudio/test/using-the-microsoft-unit-test-framework-for-testing-in-isolation) class to enforce an assumption about the expected range of the index. This assumption can be used by Pex as a precondition which would ensure that Pex will only consider execution paths that could make sense for our index and thus reduce the number of values generated and thus test execution time. The method is annotated with [PexMethod](/visualstudio/test/using-the-microsoft-pex-extensions-for-microsoft-code-quality) attribute to specify the target method and its parameters.

In the actual assertion, we use Microsoft Visual Studio’s Assert.AreEqual, to compare the original and the new state values before and after modification. By doing this, you can ensure that the array modifies correctly when you assign a new value at a certain position. The Pex test will verify this by generating the execution paths and the values of parameters for all the array’s modifications. 

In this example, the Pex test is showing the usage of the assume for a very important index value. It is used to give a precondition, which can narrow down the Pex search space. It shows how to use Pex for verification. If we did not use assume, Pex would uncover that the array does not have the index value in the first place, and it would provide incorrect results. This kind of testing is critical for a more complete automated verification.  





















