and here how it should be:

```
[TestMethod]
[PexMethod]
public void PUT_SetContract([PexIn] IList<int> list, [PexAssumeInRange(0, 100)] int val, [PexAssumeInRange(0, 100)] int index)
{
    Assert.IsTrue(index >= 0 && index <= list.Count);

    var oldValue = list[index];

    list[index] = val;
    var newValue = list[index];

    Assert.AreEqual(val, newValue);
    Assert.AreEqual(val, list.Last((item, i) => i == index));
}
```

As you can see, instead of dealing with ArrayList directly, you deal with a generic IList<T> and you have the added benefit of all the generic list methods like Add(), Remove(), Contains() being easily testable via Pex. Also, you can directly add the [PexAssumeInRange] attribute to your parameters to better control the values under test.

