##### CUT 
ArrayList Contract Tests - PUT - Set Contract - The list indexer

A Contract Test for a List's indexer (PUT - Set). 

This test targets the contract of the List's indexer (PUT - Set). The test verifies that an item can be set to a new value by using the indexer. This test also verifies that no other tests interfere with the proper operation of the List's indexer (PUT - Set).

Given a list and an item to put and an index, the test records the existing state of the list (count, item at index, index of item, last index of item, contains item) before and after the put via the indexer. It also checks for the correct count, index of the set item and last index of the set item in the list.

The PexSettings Attribute [PexMethod(VerifierSettings = "CodeContracts")], is used in order to use the Code Contracts verifier for this method. The VerifierSettings can be changed to "FxCop" or "Parasitic". However, there is not currently a VerifierSettings available for "CCTester". As a result, the ContractTest framework does not apply Code Contracts automatically, therefore, the Contracts need to be adhered to manually - either by manual creation of Contracts or by creating a ContractTest class with Contract contracts created manually.

The test checks that changes are effectively reflected by comparing the old list state before the change, and the new list state after the change. It also checks that the value in the list is the one that was set - and not whatever value was in the index location before the change.

After the put operation, the test verifies if the changes are reflected in the list: If the changed index is the same as the index where the set value was last found, then it implies success and the test has passed.

Otherwise, the test fails showing that the change was not properly reflected in our List. 

Since the test is based on contracts and not on actual implementation, the behavior of the List in an actual scenario might be different from the test. The code contracts must be written as per the expected behavior of the List.

Note: 
- The PexAssumeUnderTest Attribute above the method declaration means that the unit tests explore all branches of if statements, switch statements, and loop iterations in the method. It also tests the method itself when used normally.
- A test may not halt the execution because of an assertion error in Debug mode with an error dialog. To stop the execution, click Debug -> Exit Debugging (Managed only).

